1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,200
So the question I keep coming back to is why our onboarding takes six weeks.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:08,900
New engineers spend the first week just getting access to the systems they need.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,400
The second week they read documentation that is, um, mostly out of date.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,000
And by week three they are pairing, which is where they actually learn anything.

5
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:23,600
So what if we started pairing on day one and fixed the access problem in parallel?

6
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:28,300
The documentation could be written by the new people as they go, because they see the gaps.

7
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:33,000
Every new hire would leave the onboarding docs a little better than they found them.

8
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:38,500
That turns the most confused person in the room into the most useful editor of the docs.

9
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:43,000
I think the managers worry that pairing on day one slows down the senior engineers.

10
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:48,200
But the senior engineers already lose hours answering the same access questions in chat.

11
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:53,900
If we measured those hours I bet pairing would come out cheaper, you know, by a lot.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
So maybe the experiment is two new hires, pairing from day one, and we count the chat questions.

13
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:04,000
If the chat questions drop and the docs improve, we roll it out to the whole team next quarter.
